<!--
  <summary>
  Lists screen (custom window 1151): named media lists that port into the
  queue.
  </summary>
  <remarks>
  Left column (list 50) is one row per list, right column (list 51) the
  items of whichever list is selected on the left. Both are static lists
  bound to Home window properties published by service.py:update_lists
  (Lists.N.* and LI.N.*), the same pattern as the Favourites screen: the
  window is skin-owned so Kodi never binds its own content into it.

  Selection follows focus. Each left row carries an idx property and the
  service reads Container(50).ListItem.Property(idx) every tick while this
  window is up, so moving the cursor fills the right column; clicking a row
  jumps into its items. Clicking an item asks the service for its action
  menu (play now, move up, move down, remove). The footer buttons act on the
  selected list through Skin.String(list_command): new, rename, delete,
  port, port_shuffle, port_time.

  The action row sits on a 120px band at the foot of the screen, or at the
  top just under the header when Skin.HasSetting(lists_actions_top) is on
  (Lists and Favourites settings). Nothing is laid out twice: the band, its
  divider and the button grouplist slide up on a time="0" conditional
  animation, and both columns slide down 120px to make room. Navigation
  needs no second wiring because up and down out of either list already
  land on the grouplist, and the grouplist returns to the list, whichever
  side of the columns it is on.

  Porting copies the list into Kodi's video and/or music queue and starts it
  playing. Shuffled means the order is randomised before queueing, so the
  queue itself holds the jumbled order and Kodi's own shuffle stays off.

  Port by Time queues a random pick instead of the whole list, enough of it
  to fill a length the user gives as HH:MM. The length is remembered per
  list in lists.json and shown on the button, and the pick overshoots
  rather than falls short: the item that crosses the target is kept.
  </remarks>
-->
